    rdfs:label "A coinbase output cannot be spent until 100 blocks have been built on top of its block, a rule that the book states when it describes the first transaction of a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book explains proof of work with a toy: add a counter, the nonce, to the phrase 'Hello, world!' and hash until the result starts with a zero (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A coinbase output cannot be spent until 100 blocks have been built on top of its block, a rule that the book states when it describes the first transaction of a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "ASICBoost is a technique for constructing the block header specially so that the hardware does less work per hash (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q5_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A block has room for only 4,000,000 weight units, so a node fills the template with the transactions that pay the most for the room they take, measured as the fee rate in satoshis per virtual byte, as the chapter on fees ..."@en .

pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A block has room for only 4,000,000 weight units, so a node fills the template with the transactions that pay the most for the room they take, measured as the fee rate in satoshis per virtual byte, as the chapter on fees ..."@en .

pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "ASICBoost is a technique for constructing the block header specially so that the hardware does less work per hash (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q6_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A coinbase output cannot be spent until 100 blocks have been built on top of its block, a rule that the book states when it describes the first transaction of a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A coinbase output cannot be spent until 100 blocks have been built on top of its block, a rule that the book states when it describes the first transaction of a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A block has room for only 4,000,000 weight units, so a node fills the template with the transactions that pay the most for the room they take, measured as the fee rate in satoshis per virtual byte, as the chapter on fees ..."@en .

pg:Q7_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "ASICBoost is a technique for constructing the block header specially so that the hardware does less work per hash (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A block has room for only 4,000,000 weight units, so a node fills the template with the transactions that pay the most for the room they take, measured as the fee rate in satoshis per virtual byte, as the chapter on fees ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "ASICBoost is a technique for constructing the block header specially so that the hardware does less work per hash (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q8_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A coinbase output cannot be spent until 100 blocks have been built on top of its block, a rule that the book states when it describes the first transaction of a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "ASICBoost is a technique for constructing the block header specially so that the hardware does less work per hash (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A block has room for only 4,000,000 weight units, so a node fills the template with the transactions that pay the most for the room they take, measured as the fee rate in satoshis per virtual byte, as the chapter on fees ..."@en .

pg:Q9_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A coinbase output cannot be spent until 100 blocks have been built on top of its block, a rule that the book states when it describes the first transaction of a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:SectionTree a ipo:TreeNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Section tree"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "The section tree lists the sections of the chapter by their three levels, and choosing one opens that single section."@en .

pg:Sidebar a ipo:Sidebar,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter explorer"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "The explorer at the left shows the chapter's sections as a tree and keeps pace with the tabs of the main area."@en .

pg:SourceDocument a ipo:SourceDocument,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter 12 Stage 3 document"@en ;
    ipo:hasLearningObjective pg:CO1,
        pg:CO2,
        pg:CO3,
        pg:CO4,
        pg:CO5,
        pg:CO6 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "The Stage 3 document of chapter 12 is the text this page renders, section for section."@en .

pg:TopMenu a ipo:PrimaryNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main menu"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main menu is permanently visible at the top and lets the reader reach every top-level area without scrolling or opening anything first."@en .

pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book mentions in a single sentence the unanticipated hard fork of the upgrade from Bitcoin Core 0.7 to 0.8, caused by a limitation of the Berkeley DB used to store blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); the dates and ..."@en .

pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The reward that a miner may claim in the first transaction of a block, the subsidy, starts at 50 coins and is cut in half every 210,000 blocks, which the book describes as about every four years (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Because the subsidy is an integer number of satoshis that is halved by a shift, it reaches 1 satoshi in the thirty-third period and zero after that, so the total can be added up exactly (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Before a miner puts a transaction in a block, and before any node relays it, the node applies a fixed list of checks to it, which the book lists as the first part of independent verification (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q4_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A miner does not build a block from nothing: the node hands over a template, a list of everything the miner needs except the work, through the RPC getblocktemplate that BIP 22 defines (Dashjr, 2012)."@en .

pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book explains proof of work with a toy: add a counter, the nonce, to the phrase 'Hello, world!' and hash until the result starts with a zero (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book compares the hunt for a hash to a game with two dice, in which the players throw until someone gets a throw less than a target, and a lower target makes winning harder (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The nonce of the header is 4 bytes, so a miner can try 4,294,967,296 values before the header is used up and a new one is needed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The first solution was to use the coinbase script as extra room: eight bytes of extra nonce added to the 4-byte nonce give 2 to the 96 possible headers, a number the book writes as 8 followed by 28 zeros (Antonopoulos and ..."@en .

pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "In a hard fork, nodes with new rules accept blocks that the old nodes reject, and the two groups follow different chains (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_AsicBoostCovert a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Covert ASICBoost"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AsicBoostCovert" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_AsicBoostCovert> ;
    skos:definition "Covert ASICBoost is a hidden hardware shortcut in the search for a hash; the book recounts that segwit interfered with it, so that miners who used it would have lost their advantage if segwit were activated, which is one reason given for the dispute of 2017."@en .

pg:S_Bip34Activation a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIP 34 activation"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip34Activation" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Bip34Activation> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 34 activated by counting: when 750 of the last 1,000 blocks were version 2 invalid version-2 blocks were refused, and at 950 all version-1 blocks; the heights are now fixed in the code, 227,931 on mainnet and 21,111 on testnet3."@en .

pg:S_Bip34HeightPush a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIP 34 height push"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip34HeightPush" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Bip34HeightPush> ;
    skos:definition "Since BIP 34 the coinbase script begins with the block height as a minimally encoded number: height 2204 was written 029c08, a push of 2 bytes holding 0x089c in little-endian order, and the rule has been active on mainnet since block 227,931."@en .

pg:S_Bip54ConsensusCleanup a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIP 54 consensus cleanup"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip54ConsensusCleanup" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Bip54ConsensusCleanup> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 54, the consensus cleanup, proposes to fix the time warp attack, to invalidate a transaction with more than 2,500 signature operations over its inputs, to forbid transactions of exactly 64 bytes and to set the coinbase locktime to the height minus 1, but it is not active: the release notes of Bitcoin Core 30.0 speak of preparing for a possible BIP 54 deployment in the future."@en .

pg:S_Bip8LockInByHeight a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIP 8 lock-in by height"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip8LockInByHeight" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Bip8LockInByHeight> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 8 counts in blocks and not in time, and has an option lockinontimeout that forces the lock-in at the end, a design made after the segwit episode."@en .

pg:S_Bip94Branch a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The BIP 94 branch"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip94Branch" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Bip94Branch> ;
    skos:definition "The retarget code of Bitcoin Core 31.1 contains a branch if (params.enforce_BIP94) that the book's listing lacks; it is true for testnet4 and false for mainnet, and BIP 94 describes the time warp prevention in which it belongs."@en .

pg:S_Bip9StateMachine a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The BIP 9 state machine"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip9StateMachine" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Bip9StateMachine> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 9 gives each deployment one of the states defined, started, locked_in, active or failed, decided once per period of 2016 blocks with a threshold of 1,916 (95%) on bits 0 to 28; on regtest, periods of 144 and a threshold of 108 gave defined at 143, started at 144, locked_in at 288 and active at 432."@en .

pg:S_BitcoinCashFork2017 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Bitcoin Cash fork of 2017"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BitcoinCashFork2017" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_BitcoinCashFork2017> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Cash split from Bitcoin on 1 August 2017 at block 478,559, the last common block being 478,558, a hard fork that the book names and that kept the 2016-block difficulty rule but added an emergency adjustment."@en .

pg:S_BlockChecklist a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block checklist"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockChecklist" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_BlockChecklist> ;
    skos:definition "Every node also applies a checklist to a block; fourteen blocks built from bytes on the test chain were each given one verdict: a wrong merkle root, a missing proof of work (high-hash), a time not after the median, a time two hours and a second ahead, a reward one satoshi too high, no height, a version of 1, wrong difficulty bits, coinbase scripts of 1 and 101 bytes, a second coinbase, no coinbase, a block over the weight limit, and the valid block, which was accepted."@en .

pg:S_BlockTemplate a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block template"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockTemplate" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_BlockTemplate> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the template that a node gives to a miner and the order in which it fills a block with transactions (Dashjr, 2012)."@en .

pg:S_BlockWithholding a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block withholding"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockWithholding" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_BlockWithholding> ;
    skos:definition "A member of a pool can hand in shares but keep back a block that would win, damaging the pool without gaining a reward; Optech describes the attack and the book's pool section does not."@en .

pg:S_BookScriptFloatErratum a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The float erratum of the book's script"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BookScriptFloatErratum" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_BookScriptFloatErratum> ;
    skos:definition "The script max_money.py of the book divides the reward with current_reward /= 2, which is a float division in Python 3, so run as printed it goes on halving into fractions for 1,107 steps until the float underflows to zero and prints 2100000000000000.0 and not the 2099999997690000 of the book; with the integer division //= the result of the book appears."@en .

pg:S_BuildingTheCandidateBlock a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Building the candidate block"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BuildingTheCandidateBlock" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_BuildingTheCandidateBlock> ;
    skos:definition "The third subject is how a miner assembles a block from the template, the coinbase and the header before any hashing is done (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_BuriedDeployments a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Buried deployments"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BuriedDeployments" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_BuriedDeployments> ;
    skos:definition "Once a soft fork is old, the code stores only its height: a strict test node reported BIP 34 as buried and active from height 1, a lax one as buried at height 100,000, and mainnet's segwit height is the plain number 481,824 instead of a state machine."@en .

pg:S_ChainSelection a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chain selection"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChainSelection" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ChainSelection> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about choosing between competing chains by most work and about what happens to a payment when the chain that holds it is replaced (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ChainSplitMarch2013 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The chain split of March 2013"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChainSplitMarch2013" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ChainSplitMarch2013> ;
    skos:definition "On 2013-03-11 at 23:24:19 UTC block 225,430, with 797 transactions and 249,119 bytes, split the chain because Bitcoin 0.8 accepted it and older versions rejected it (the Berkeley DB lock limit of the older versions had become an accidental consensus rule); the split ended 24 blocks later, at block 225,454 at 06:19:32 UTC on 2013-03-12."@en .

pg:S_ChangingConsensusRules a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Changing consensus rules"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChangingConsensusRules" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ChangingConsensusRules> ;
    skos:definition "The sixth subject is how the rules can be changed without breaking the network: hard forks that need everybody and soft forks that need only the miners, with the signalling schemes that decide when (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ClampFactorFour a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The factor-of-four clamp"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ClampFactorFour" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ClampFactorFour> ;
    skos:definition "The measured timespan is clamped between a quarter and four times the two weeks: a period of 10 times too long gave a target exactly 4 times larger (0x170d0864), a period 10 times too short a target 0.25 times as large (0x1700d086), and the code lines are in pow.cpp."@en .

pg:S_CoinbaseDataAndTags a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase data and tags"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoinbaseDataAndTags" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_CoinbaseDataAndTags> ;
    skos:definition "The free data of the coinbase carries the pool's tag: block 840,000 has /ViaBTC/ and 'Mined by buzz120', block 709,632 /F2Pool/ and block 277,316 /P2SH/, of 96, 100 and 15 bytes, within the limit of 2 to 100 bytes, and the test chain refused scripts of 1 and 101 bytes."@en .

pg:S_CoinbaseInputIsNull a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase input is null"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoinbaseInputIsNull" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_CoinbaseInputIsNull> ;
    skos:definition "The coinbase has exactly one input whose previous output is 32 zero bytes and index ffffffff, so it spends nothing; on the test chain its script was 5100, height 1 followed by the OP_0 of the new software, and its sequence was 4294967294."@en .

pg:S_CoinbaseMaturity a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase maturity"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoinbaseMaturity" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_CoinbaseMaturity> ;
    skos:definition "A coinbase output can be spent only 100 blocks later, the constant COINBASE_MATURITY: a transaction spending it was refused with bad-txns-premature-spend-of-coinbase at depth 1 and at depth 99, and accepted at depth 100, so that a reorganization cannot orphan a spent reward."@en .

pg:S_CoinbaseTransaction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoinbaseTransaction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_CoinbaseTransaction> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the first transaction of the block: its null input, the height, the locktime, the witness commitment, the tags and the extra nonce (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_CompactBits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compact bits"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CompactBits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_CompactBits> ;
    skos:definition "The 4-byte bits field stores a target as an exponent byte and three bytes of mantissa: 0x1903a30c is 0x03a30c times 256 to the power 22, converting back gives 0x1903a30c, a mantissa with its high bit set is shifted (0x00800000 becomes 0x04008000) and 0x1d00ffff expands to the difficulty-1 target."@en .

pg:S_CoreCoinbaseFieldsToday a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase fields of Bitcoin Core today"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreCoinbaseFieldsToday" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_CoreCoinbaseFieldsToday> ;
    skos:definition "The coinbase transaction of Bitcoin Core 31.1 sets nLockTime to the height minus 1 and nSequence to 0xfffffffe, and ends its script with OP_0; the test coinbase at height 2204 had locktime 2203, and the book describes none of these fields."@en .

pg:S_DecentralizedConsensus a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Decentralized consensus"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DecentralizedConsensus" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_DecentralizedConsensus> ;
    skos:definition "The second subject is why nobody has to be trusted: every node checks every transaction and block by the same rules, picks the chain with the most work and rejects the rest (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_DiceAnalogy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Dice analogy"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DiceAnalogy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_DiceAnalogy> ;
    skos:definition "In the dice game of the book a throw of two dice wins if it is at most the target: the target 3 wins in 3 of 36 throws (8.33%), 4 in 6 of 36 and 8 in 26 of 36, so a lower target means a harder win, as with a hash."@en .

pg:S_DifficultyFromTarget a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Difficulty from target"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DifficultyFromTarget" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_DifficultyFromTarget> ;
    skos:definition "Difficulty is the ratio of the easiest target (0x1d00ffff, difficulty 1) to the block's target: bits 1903a30c give 1,180,923,195 for block 277,316, equal to what mempool.space lists, and block 840,000 had 86,388,558,925,171."@en .

pg:S_DoubleSpendByReorganization a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Double spend by reorganization"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DoubleSpendByReorganization" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_DoubleSpendByReorganization> ;
    skos:definition "In the test, Mallory's payment of 49.999 coins to Carol had 1 confirmation and Carol had received 49.999; then a competing block with a refund of the same coins was added with one block more of work, the payment went to -2 confirmations (conflicted), Carol had received 0 and the refund had 2 confirmations."@en .

pg:S_ExtraNonceChangesRoot a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The extra nonce changes the root"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ExtraNonceChangesRoot" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ExtraNonceChangesRoot> ;
    skos:definition "Any change to the coinbase script changes its txid, which changes the merkle root and so the whole header hash: in the test two coinbases that differed only in extra nonce gave different txids, merkle roots and hashes with the same header nonce."@en .

pg:S_ExtraNonceSpace a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The extra nonce space"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ExtraNonceSpace" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ExtraNonceSpace> ;
    skos:definition "An 8-byte extra nonce in the coinbase and the 4-byte nonce give 2 to the 96, 7.9e28, headers to try without touching the timestamp, a number with 29 digits that the book writes as 8 followed by 28 zeros."@en .

pg:S_FailedDeployment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Failed deployment"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FailedDeployment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_FailedDeployment> ;
    skos:definition "A deployment whose time ran out without enough signals goes from started to failed at the end of its period (height 288 in the test) and stays failed for ever."@en .

pg:S_FeeRateOrdering a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee rate ordering"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeRateOrdering" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_FeeRateOrdering> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core fills a template from the mempool by fee rate: four transactions of 200, 50, 10 and 2 satoshis per virtual byte entered the block in exactly that order, the highest payer first."@en .

pg:S_FeesOfBlock840000 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The fees of block 840,000"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeesOfBlock840000" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_FeesOfBlock840000> ;
    skos:definition "The block that began the fourth era, number 840,000, mined by ViaBTC, carried 3,762,561,499 satoshis of fees against a subsidy of 312,500,000, 12.04 times the subsidy, in 3,050 transactions, so the book's remark that fees are usually a small percentage of income was false for that block."@en .

pg:S_GreedyCoinbaseRejected a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Greedy coinbase rejected"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GreedyCoinbaseRejected" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_GreedyCoinbaseRejected> ;
    skos:definition "A coinbase that claims one satoshi more than subsidy plus fees makes the block invalid: the node answered bad-cb-amount, the string that validation.cpp contains, both when the block was built from the template and when it was built by hand."@en .

pg:S_HalvingEvery210000 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Halving every 210,000 blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HalvingEvery210000" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HalvingEvery210000> ;
    skos:definition "The subsidy is 50 coins shifted right by one bit for every 210,000 blocks: blocks 210,000, 420,000, 630,000 and 840,000 were mined on 2012-11-28, 2016-07-09, 2020-05-11 and 2024-04-20 and carry 25, 12.5, 6.25 and 3.125 coins, each half of the block before it, and the interval is the constant nSubsidyHalvingInterval of 210,000."@en .

pg:S_HardForks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hard forks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HardForks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HardForks> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about changes that split the network: the arithmetic of a minority chain, the accidental fork of 2013 and the deliberate fork of 2017 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_HashBelowTarget a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash below target"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashBelowTarget" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HashBelowTarget> ;
    skos:definition "Block 277,316 had to hash below a target of 194 bits, so its hash needed at least 62 leading zero bits, and it has 63, hash 0000000000000001b6b9a13b...; the book says 'more than 60'."@en .

pg:S_HashrateAttacks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hashrate attacks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashrateAttacks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HashrateAttacks> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the attacks that need a large share of the hash rate and how likely they are to succeed at each share (Nakamoto, 2008)."@en .

pg:S_HashrateFromDifficulty a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hash rate from difficulty"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashrateFromDifficulty" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HashrateFromDifficulty> ;
    skos:definition "Difficulty times 2 to the 32 divided by 600 seconds estimates the hash rate: 132,716,002,350,731 gives 9.5e20 hashes per second, 8% under the 1.029e21 of mempool.space, and 1.003e21 on blockchain.com, 121,738 times what the difficulty of block 277,316 implied."@en .

pg:S_HeaderConstruction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Header construction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeaderConstruction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HeaderConstruction> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the 80-byte header that is hashed: its fields in a real block, the version bits and the two rules for the time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_HeaderOfBlock277316 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Header of block277316"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeaderOfBlock277316" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HeaderOfBlock277316> ;
    skos:definition "The header of the book's example block 277,316, mined at 2013-12-27 23:11:54 UTC, parses to version 2, bits 1903a30c and nonce 924,591,752, hashes to the block id 0000000000000001b6b9a13b..., contains the merkle root of the block and meets its own target."@en .

pg:S_HelloWorldNonce a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Hello World nonce"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HelloWorldNonce" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_HelloWorldNonce> ;
    skos:definition "Adding a counter to a phrase until the hash starts with a zero first succeeds at the counter 32, whose hash begins 09cb91f8, as the book prints; two zeros need 228 and three zeros 3,460, and the book's prose 'Hello, World! 32' with a capital W gives a different hash (6bbe6a3d), a typing slip, because the shell example uses a lowercase w."@en .

pg:S_IndependentVerification a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Independent verification"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#IndependentVerification" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_IndependentVerification> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the checklists: the transaction rules, the block rules and the maturity of rewards, each tested with a rule broken on purpose (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_MajorityAttackLimits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The limits of a majority attack"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MajorityAttackLimits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MajorityAttackLimits> ;
    skos:definition "The book says that a majority attacker can double-spend his own transactions and can censor chosen transactions by re-mining blocks that include them, and that the attack can be attempted with less than 51%; the odds computed above agree."@en .

pg:S_MedianTimePast a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Median time past"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MedianTimePast" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MedianTimePast> ;
    skos:definition "The time of a block must be greater than the median of the previous 11 blocks (BIP 113): the test chain refused a block whose time equalled the median (time-too-old), the median of 1 to 11 shuffled is 6, and block 277,316 was 2,239 seconds after its median time past."@en .

pg:S_MinerMayClaimLess a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Miner may claim less"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MinerMayClaimLess" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MinerMayClaimLess> ;
    skos:definition "A miner may leave part of the reward unclaimed: on the test chain a block whose coinbase took 345,368 satoshis, one less than the allowed 345,369, was accepted, and the satoshi never exists, which is the 'lower total issuance' that the book mentions."@en .

pg:S_MiningCompetition a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mining competition"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MiningCompetition" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MiningCompetition> ;
    skos:definition "The fifth subject is the contest among miners: how they get enough room to vary the header, how they join in pools, and how large a share of the hash rate would be needed to attack (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MiningPools a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mining pools"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MiningPools" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MiningPools> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about pools: how concentrated they are, why small miners join, how shares are counted, which protocols they speak and what attacks them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MinorityChainSlow a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The minority chain is slow"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MinorityChainSlow" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MinorityChainSlow> ;
    skos:definition "If 80% follow new rules after a hard fork, their chain gets a block every 12.5 minutes and needs 17.5 days (25,200 minutes) for 2016 blocks before the difficulty drops, while the 20% chain gets a block every 50 minutes and needs 70 days."@en .

pg:S_MoneyCreation a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Money creation"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MoneyCreation" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MoneyCreation> ;
    skos:definition "The first subject is the way new coins come into being: a reward that is cut in half on a fixed timetable and the fees of the transactions, claimed in the first transaction of each block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MostWorkNotLongest a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Most work, not longest"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MostWorkNotLongest" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_MostWorkNotLongest> ;
    skos:definition "Nodes follow the valid chain with the most cumulative work, not the one with the most blocks: after the test double spend the winning tip had chainwork 208 against 206, a tie in work (two blocks at height 102) left the first-seen block as the tip and the other as valid-headers, and the book states the rule as 'the most cumulative PoW'."@en .

pg:S_NakamotoCatchUpOdds a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Nakamoto's catch-up odds"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NakamotoCatchUpOdds" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_NakamotoCatchUpOdds> ;
    skos:definition "An attacker with 10% of the hash rate catches up from 6 blocks behind with probability 0.0002428 and from 1 block with 0.2045873; with 30% the figure for 6 blocks is 0.1321, with 45% it is 0.766 and with half it is nearly certain, recomputed with Nakamoto's formula."@en .

pg:S_NextAdjustmentToday a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Next adjustment today"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NextAdjustmentToday" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_NextAdjustmentToday> ;
    skos:definition "On 2026-10-15 the tip was at height 971,561 and the next retarget, at 971,712 (a multiple of 2016), was estimated at plus 6.44%, after a previous change of minus 3.09%."@en .

pg:S_NonceSpace a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Nonce space"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NonceSpace" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_NonceSpace> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about running out of nonces and the three ways round it: the extra nonce, the rolled version bits and the tricks of the hardware (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_NonceSpaceIsTiny a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The nonce space is tiny"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NonceSpaceIsTiny" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_NonceSpaceIsTiny> ;
    skos:definition "The 4-byte nonce has 4,294,967,296 values, which today's network of 1.029e21 hashes per second would use up in 4.17 picoseconds, so the nonce alone cannot keep a machine busy, and the network runs through 2.4e11 nonce spaces each second."@en .

pg:S_PoolConcentration a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pool concentration"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PoolConcentration" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_PoolConcentration> ;
    skos:definition "In the last year, of 52,371 blocks, Foundry USA mined 14,750 (28.2%), AntPool 9,510 (18.2%), F2Pool 6,602 (12.6%) and ViaBTC 5,077 (9.7%), so two pools found 46.3% and four 68.6% of the blocks, out of 32 named pools."@en .

pg:S_PoolSharesAndFees a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pool shares and fees"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PoolSharesAndFees" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_PoolSharesAndFees> ;
    skos:definition "A pool sets an easier target so that members prove their work with shares: with one in 1,024 hashes being a share, a miner expects a thousand shares for each block, and the pool pays by the share and takes a fee."@en .

pg:S_ProofIsCheapToCheck a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Proof is cheap to check"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ProofIsCheapToCheck" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ProofIsCheapToCheck> ;
    skos:definition "Finding a hash below a target takes many attempts and checking it takes one: on the test chain the search at bits 1e0fffff needed 1,031,915 attempts (expected 1,048,577), at 1f00ffff 72,470 (expected 65,537) and at regtest difficulty 1, and the winning hash was verified by one comparison."@en .

pg:S_ProofOfWork a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Proof of work"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ProofOfWork" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_ProofOfWork> ;
    skos:definition "The fourth subject is the work itself: a hash below a target, a target that follows from a compact number, and a difficulty that adjusts every 2,016 blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_RecentAdjustments a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Recent adjustments"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RecentAdjustments" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_RecentAdjustments> ;
    skos:definition "The last 52 retargets, 2024-10-22 to 2026-10-03, changed the difficulty between 0.888 and 1.147 times, 20 of them downward, and the difficulty rose by 1.39 times in all."@en .

pg:S_RegtestHalvesEvery150 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Regtest halves every 150 blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RegtestHalvesEvery150" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_RegtestHalvesEvery150> ;
    skos:definition "On regtest the halving interval is 150, so the test chain showed 50 coins at heights 1 and 149, 25 at 150 and 299, 12.5 at 300 and 449 and 6.25 at 450, and at height 2,000 the reward had shrunk to 610,351 satoshis, the value of the same shift rule."@en .

pg:S_RegtestNeverRetargets a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Regtest never retargets"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RegtestNeverRetargets" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_RegtestNeverRetargets> ;
    skos:definition "Regtest sets fPowNoRetargeting, so the test chain stayed at bits 207fffff at heights 1, 2015, 2016, 2017 and 2100, and a real retarget by Bitcoin Core was not executed; mainnet has the flag false."@en .

pg:S_RetargetFormulaOnRealEpoch a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Retarget formula on real epoch"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RetargetFormulaOnRealEpoch" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_RetargetFormulaOnRealEpoch> ;
    skos:definition "Applying the rule of pow.cpp to the first and last blocks of the period ending at 840,671, 1,186,047 seconds apart, and its bits 17034219 gives 170331db, the bits that block 840,672 really carries; the period was 1.02 times faster than the 1,209,600 seconds aimed at."@en .

pg:S_Retargeting a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Retargeting"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Retargeting" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Retargeting> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the adjustment every 2,016 blocks, tested against a real epoch, with its flaws, its limits and its newest branch (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_RewardComposition a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Reward composition"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RewardComposition" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_RewardComposition> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about what the reward is made of, the subsidy and the fees, and about the rule that the sum may be claimed in full, partly, but never above the limit (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_RewardIsSubsidyPlusFees a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Reward is subsidy plus fees"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RewardIsSubsidyPlusFees" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_RewardIsSubsidyPlusFees> ;
    skos:definition "The most a coinbase may claim is the subsidy plus the fees of the block: on the test chain a template with four transactions had a coinbase value of 345,369 satoshis, 305,175 of subsidy and 40,194 of fees, and real block 277,316 had a reward of 2,509,094,928 satoshis, 25 coins and 9,094,928 satoshis of fees."@en .

pg:S_Segwit2017Activation a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Segwit activation in 2017"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Segwit2017Activation" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_Segwit2017Activation> ;
    skos:definition "Segwit was deployed in 2017 with a BIP 9 deployment that stalled; BIP 91 lowered the threshold and BIP 148 would have made nodes reject blocks that did not signal, after which miners signalled, and the rules are active from block 481,824 (CSV from 419,328)."@en .

pg:S_SelfishMiningThreshold a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The selfish-mining threshold"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SelfishMiningThreshold" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_SelfishMiningThreshold> ;
    skos:definition "Eyal and Sirer showed that holding back blocks pays above (1 - g) / (3 - 2g) of the hash rate, where g is the share of honest miners who follow the selfish block: 1/3 for g = 0, 1/4 for g = 1/2 and 0 for g = 1."@en .

pg:S_SoftForkOnlyTightens a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A soft fork only tightens"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SoftForkOnlyTightens" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_SoftForkOnlyTightens> ;
    skos:definition "A soft fork makes rules stricter: on a pair of test nodes the lax node accepted a block with no height in its coinbase and the strict node answered bad-cb-height, the strict node could not follow the lax chain (prev-blk-not-found), and the lax node reorganized onto the longer strict chain; both rejected version 1."@en .

pg:S_SoftForks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Soft forks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SoftForks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_SoftForks> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about changes that keep old nodes on the chain, and the schemes used to decide when to switch them on: counting, BIP 9, BIP 8 and Speedy Trial (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_SoloMinerVariance a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Solo miner variance"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SoloMinerVariance" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_SoloMinerVariance> ;
    skos:definition "A miner with 1% of the hash rate would average 1.44 blocks a day, as the book says (more than one), but one with 0.01% averages 5.26 blocks a year, waits 69 days on average, and has a 0.52% chance of seeing none in the year, which is why small miners join pools."@en .

pg:S_SpeedyTrialTaproot a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Speedy Trial and taproot"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SpeedyTrialTaproot" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_SpeedyTrialTaproot> ;
    skos:definition "Taproot was activated with Speedy Trial: start 2021-04-24, timeout 2021-08-11, a threshold of 1,815 of 2,016 (90%), and a minimum activation height of 709,632; block 709,632 was mined by F2Pool on 2021-11-14 05:15:27 UTC."@en .

pg:S_StratumProtocols a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Stratum protocols"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#StratumProtocols" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_StratumProtocols> ;
    skos:definition "Pools talk to machines in the Stratum protocol, which BIP 310 extends with version rolling, while Stratum V2 encrypts the channel and optionally lets miners declare their own block templates, and Bitcoin Core 30.0 added an experimental IPC mining interface for Stratum v2 and other client software; getblocktemplate (BIP 22) is the older route."@en .

pg:S_SubsidySchedule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Subsidy schedule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SubsidySchedule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_SubsidySchedule> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the timetable of the reward: the halving every 210,000 blocks, the limit on the supply, an error in the book's script and the shortened interval of regtest (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_TargetAndDifficulty a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Target and difficulty"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TargetAndDifficulty" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TargetAndDifficulty> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the target, the way it is packed in the header, the difficulty and the hash rate that follows from it (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_TemplateFields a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Template fields"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TemplateFields" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TemplateFields> ;
    skos:definition "A block template gives the miner everything but the work: previousblockhash, height, bits and target, curtime and mintime, the coinbasevalue (5,000,000,000 satoshis at height 1), the limits weightlimit 4,000,000 and sigoplimit 80,000, the nonce range 00000000ffffffff, the rules csv, segwit and taproot, and the default witness commitment."@en .

pg:S_TerahashMinerWait a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The wait of a terahash miner"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TerahashMinerWait" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TerahashMinerWait> ;
    skos:definition "The book says that one miner doing 1 TH/s would find a block of height 277,316 once in 8,496 blocks or 59 days; the difficulty 1,180,923,195 times 2 to the 32 divided by 10 to the 12 gives 58.7 days or 8,453 blocks, about 0.5% fewer."@en .

pg:S_TheLottery a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The lottery"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TheLottery" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TheLottery> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the idea of proof of work shown with a toy hash, a pair of dice and the cost of checking compared to the cost of finding (Back, 2002)."@en .

pg:S_TopPoolVersusThresholds a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The top pool against the thresholds"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TopPoolVersusThresholds" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TopPoolVersusThresholds> ;
    skos:definition "The largest pool of the last year, Foundry USA, found 28.16% of the blocks, under 1/3 and over 1/4, and the two largest together 46%."@en .

pg:S_TotalSupplyBelow21Million a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Total supply below 21 million"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TotalSupplyBelow21Million" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TotalSupplyBelow21Million> ;
    skos:definition "Summing the 33 non-zero rewards in whole satoshis gives 2,099,999,997,690,000 satoshis, 2,310,000 satoshis (0.0231 coins) short of 21 million coins; the last block with a reward of 1 satoshi is 6,929,999 and block 6,930,000 has none, about the year 2140, because the code shifts an integer."@en .

pg:S_TransactionChecklist a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Transaction checklist"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TransactionChecklist" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TransactionChecklist> ;
    skos:definition "Every node applies the same checklist to a transaction; on the test chain thirteen cases were run, ten of them malformed transactions refused each with its own reason (no outputs, a negative output, an output over 21 million coins, a total over 21 million coins, a repeated input, a missing input, outputs larger than inputs, a locktime in the future, a bad signature, and one with no inputs that could not even be decoded), and spending a coinbase of depth 100 was accepted."@en .

pg:S_TwoHourRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Two hour rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TwoHourRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TwoHourRule> ;
    skos:definition "A node refuses a block whose time is more than 2 hours, 7,200 seconds, ahead of its own clock, MAX_FUTURE_BLOCK_TIME: the test block 2 hours and a second ahead was answered time-too-new."@en .

pg:S_TwoThousandFifteenGaps a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015-gap retarget"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TwoThousandFifteenGaps" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_TwoThousandFifteenGaps> ;
    skos:definition "The timespan is measured from the first to the last block of a period, which are 2015 gaps and not 2016, so the retargeting aims at a block every 9.995 minutes, 0.05% faster than 10; the code takes the first block as height minus 2015 and the difference of the last block's time."@en .

pg:S_VersionHistory a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Version history"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#VersionHistory" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_VersionHistory> ;
    skos:definition "The version field was 2 in block 277,316 (BIP 34), 0x20200004 in block 709,632, which signals bit 2 for taproot, and 0x2a5fe000 in block 840,000, where the top three bits are 001 and the bits 0x0a5fe000 carry rolled data."@en .

pg:S_VersionRolling a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Version rolling"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#VersionRolling" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_VersionRolling> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 320 reserves the 16 bits of mask 0x1fffe000 for rolling: 65,536 values times 2 to the 32 nonces is 2 to the 48 hashes per header, which is 281.5 TH/s if a machine finishes a header each second, the book's 281; a block with rolled version 0x2000e000 was accepted at height 3 of the test chain."@en .

pg:S_WitnessCommitment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Witness commitment"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WitnessCommitment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12#S_WitnessCommitment> ;
    skos:definition "A block with segwit transactions commits to all witnesses in a coinbase output that begins 6a24aa21a9ed: the 32-byte commitment is the double SHA-256 of the witness merkle root and 32 reserved zero bytes, the test recomputed the value in the block from the five wtxids, and the first wtxid is 32 zero bytes."@en .

pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Describe the checklists every node applies to a transaction and to a block, and explain why the chain with the most work, not the longest, is the best chain"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can describe the checklists every node applies to a transaction and to a block, and explain why the chain with the most work, not the longest, is the best chain."@en .

pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Build a candidate block: choose transactions from the template by fee rate, write the coinbase transaction and its fields, and fill in the header"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can build a candidate block: choose transactions from the template by fee rate, write the coinbase transaction and its fields, and fill in the header."@en .

pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compute the block subsidy at any height from the halving every 210,000 blocks, show why the total supply stays below 21 million, and say what a miner may claim as reward (subsidy plus fees)"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compute the block subsidy at any height from the halving every 210,000 blocks, show why the total supply stays below 21 million, and say what a miner may claim as reward (subsidy plus fees)."@en .

pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain proof of work as a lottery of hashes below a target, convert between target, bits, difficulty and hash rate, and apply the retargeting formula every 2,016 blocks"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain proof of work as a lottery of hashes below a target, convert between target, bits, difficulty and hash rate, and apply the retargeting formula every 2,016 blocks."@en .

pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Judge the competition among miners: the nonce and extra nonce space, the pools and their shares, and the limits of selfish-mining and majority attacks"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Evaluate ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can judge the competition among miners: the nonce and extra nonce space, the pools and their shares, and the limits of selfish-mining and majority attacks."@en .

pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compare hard forks and soft forks, follow a deployment through the BIP 9 state machine, and explain how taproot was activated"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch12/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compare hard forks and soft forks, follow a deployment through the BIP 9 state machine, and explain how taproot was activated."@en .

